  "type": "singleCorrect",
  "level": 1,
  "question": {
    "text": "In Bohr&#39;s atomic model of hydrogen, let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>K</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>P</mi></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>E</mi></math> are the kinetic energy, potential energy and total energy of the electron respectively. Choose the correct option when the electron undergoes transitions to a higher level :",
    "image": null
  },
  "options": [
    {
      "id": "e2Lsb",
      "text": "All&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>K</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>P</mi></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>E</mi></math> increase.",
      "isCorrect": false,
      "image": null
    },
    {
      "id": "V98Jr",
      "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>K</mi></math> decreases, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>P</mi></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>E</mi></math> increase.",
      "isCorrect": true,
      "image": null
    },
    {
      "id": "COFH0",
      "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>P</mi></math> decreases, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>K</mi></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>E</mi></math> increase.",
      "isCorrect": false,
      "image": null
    },
    {
      "id": "wnVnf",
      "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>K</mi></math> increases, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>P</mi></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>E</mi></math> decrease",
      "isCorrect": false,
      "image": null
    }
  ],
  "correctValue": null,
  "solution": {
    "text": "<p>Total energy of electron is stated as&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>E</mi><mo>=</mo><mo>-</mo><mfrac><mrow><msup><mi>Z</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>m</mi><msup><mi>e</mi><mn>4</mn></msup></mrow><mrow><mn>8</mn><msup><mfenced><mrow><mi>n</mi><mi>h</mi><msub><mi>&#949;</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></mfenced><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mfrac></math>, potential energy&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>P</mi><mo>=</mo><mo>-</mo><mfrac><mrow><msup><mi>Z</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>m</mi><msup><mi>e</mi><mn>4</mn></msup></mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn><msup><mfenced><mrow><mi>n</mi><mi>h</mi><msub><mi>&#949;</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></mfenced><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mfrac></math>&nbsp;and kinetic energy is given by&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>K</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><msup><mi>Z</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>m</mi><msup><mi>e</mi><mn>4</mn></msup></mrow><mrow><mn>8</mn><msup><mfenced><mrow><mi>n</mi><mi>h</mi><msub><mi>&#949;</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></mfenced><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mfrac></math>.</p><p>So, as electron makes transition to higher level, total energy and potential energy increases due to the negative sign while the kinetic energy of electron decreases.</p>",
    "image": null
  },
